  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Mockingjay Logo**\n\nI\'m focusing on the visual elements of the Mockingjay logo, specifically for the `PhoenixParticles.tsx` file. I\'m prioritizing the bird\'s form and the burning ring/circle, trying to capture its magnificent essence. My initial focus is on defining the bird\'s characteristics within that flaming frame. The design is intended to be a stylized phoenix.\n\n\n**Modeling the Phoenix Emblem**\n\nI\'m now diving into the mathematical modeling of the phoenix emblem. I plan to use distributions to create a visually striking burning ring. I\'m focusing on defining the radius, particle spread and noise for a fiery effect. I intend to use gold, orange-red, copper, and scarlet colors for a glowing effect. The ultimate intention is to model a visual of the bird within the ring.\n\n\n**Constructing the Phoenix**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on the phoenix\'s physical structure, defining sections for the body, wings, and tail. I\'ve started with a central spine and am now modeling the head with a slightly upward-pointing beak. Symmetrical wings are the priority, curving up and out with feathered details, all based on a parameter `t`. I\'m working on the wings\' feather layers using parallel rows/layers. The tail will be a long, cascading fan.\n\n\n**Implementing Mouse Interactions**\n\nI\'m now integrating mouse interactions. I plan to make the particles react dynamically, drawing them towards the cursor. I\'m introducing attraction and rotational forces to achieve an interactive liquid/field effect, similar to the rose animation. The core idea is a magnetic attraction and a vortex swirl centered at the cursor for that mesmerising fluid spiral. I am excited to see how this beautiful concept looks in action.\n\n\n**Refining the Emblem Particles**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the particle system for the emblem. The target is a highly polished and high-contrast look, so I\'m increasing the particle count to 18,000 and focusing on size variations for a glowing effect. I\'m reviewing position storage and animation, aiming for subtle, natural movement like heat shimmer and wing flaps, with ring rotation/sparks. When the mouse interacts, the particles will respond with an interactive vortex, returning smoothly to their initial emblem positions.\n\n\n**Defining Particle Parameters**\n\nI\'m now detailing particle parameters, specifically their sector types. I\'ve split particles into a circular ring (40%), central bird (15%), wings (25%), and tail (20%). The circular ring is defined by polar coordinates with flame flare effects. For the bird\'s body and head, I\'m using a \'t\' parameter to map a curve for its width and position, while the head has a noble upward and right-angled beak.\n\n\n**Modeling Wing Feather Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the wings\' structure, especially the feathers. I\'m focusing on creating symmetrical, majestic wings. Each wing, left and right, will have feathers stemming from a central \"bone\" curve, defined by equations for x and y. I\'m using a progress parameter \'u\' to determine each feather\'s length and angle. Feather density will increase towards the wingtip, adding a stylized, sharp look. I am very pleased with these equations.\n\n\n**Designing the Particle Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now implementing equations to define wing and tail structures with particle distributions. I\'m especially happy with the wing feather equations, which provide a majestic feathered outline with a dynamic progress parameter. For the tail, I\'m using similar equations to create symmetrical, outwards-branching streamers. I also have chosen an initial colour palette.\n\n**Refining Interaction Algorithm**\n\nI\'ve just been working on the colour palette, and testing the attraction algorithm in the animation loop. I\'m focusing on a high-contrast palette, from fiery yellows to glowing roses. I\'m currently checking the interaction algorithm and making sure that the particles correctly react.\n\n\n**Implementing Rotation & Flapping**\n\nI\'m now integrating animation to the particle loop using elapsed time. I\'m focusing on rotating the outer ring, implementing small angle rotations based on `elapsedTime`. I\'m using `sin` and `cos` for subtle wing flapping and tail streamer waves.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Force Vectors**\n\nI\'m now calculating mouse force vectors to drive particle interactions. I\'m translating screen coordinates to world coordinates to find the distance, `dist`, between particles and the cursor. Based on this distance I\'m defining a pull force, along with a swirl force for a fluid vortex effect. I\'m experimenting with different strengths to achieve a beautiful, flowing visual.\n\n\n"
