  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Bird Generation Logic**\n\nI\'m focused on creating exceptionally clean and symmetrical procedural bird generation. My aim is to perfectly capture the essence of the low-poly golden bird image. The design will be sharp, and precise.\n\n\n**Constructing Bird Anatomy**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the bird\'s anatomy. I\'ve defined a slender torso and neck with symmetrical tapering, extending up to a proud head. The idea is to build a perfect 3D particle shell for the body based on meticulously generated surface points. Wings will sweep outwards from the torso, each composed of sharp, parallel feather blades defined by a dense line of micro-particles.\n\n\n**Defining Feather Structures**\n\nI\'m now calculating wing bone positions precisely with mathematical formulas. I\'m excited to define each feather explicitly using these points, guaranteeing sharp, modeled-looking wings with dense particle lines. I\'m also planning the tail: seven distinct, long, straight blades of particles fanning downwards to perfectly capture the golden bird\'s image.\nI have also integrated the user\'s color suggestion to create a more subtle and non overwhelming look.\n\n\n**Refining Interactive Lighting**\n\nI\'m now optimizing the interactive particle lighting and feel. I\'ve set particle size to 0.012, with an almost invisible, deeply muted dark burgundy slate default color (`#040001` to `#0d0203`). The bird is barely visible until a cursor hovers, then bright neon gold or intense copper-red particles light up with generous interaction radius and glowing trails. The procedural code will be in `/src/components/PhoenixParticles.tsx`, which should have ~26000 particles to make the bird rich and sharp.\n\n\n**Calculating Feather Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now detailing the wing feather generation for a sharp, defined look. Each wing will have 15 precisely defined feathers using mathematical formulas. I\'m focusing on the wing bone curve with its `u` parameter to determine feather starting points. Feather length, determined by the formula `L`, and angle variations, will be key to capturing a natural, yet stylized, wing appearance. Each feather point will be sampled using parameters from `v` to create a smooth line.\n\n\n**Generating Tail & Body**\n\nI\'m now implementing the tail feathers with sharp, vertical designs. Each feather will be individually placed using formulas for base points and length, creating a fanned structure. The body is in progress, building a dense, smooth centerline shell to capture the bird\'s form. Tapering will create a slender, elegant torso.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I am now rewriting the `PhoenixParticles.tsx` file to structure the bird using precise mathematical splines and individual feather lines, making the shape fully recognizable, sharp, and composed entirely of ultra-fine, subtle micro-particles that glowing-ignite on interaction."
