  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Carousel Showcase**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the marquee implementation within `App.tsx`, specifically the shadow-free carousel showcase. I\'m scrutinizing its relative positioning, z-index, and height constraints to ensure seamless integration and visual harmony. The goal is a clean, lower-profile, rounded image display within the marquee structure.\n\n\n**Refining Marquee Layout**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the marquee\'s card presentation. My focus has shifted towards reducing the number of columns and enlarging the individual cards for a more impactful visual presence. I\'m experimenting with wider card dimensions within the existing framework. The aim is to create a more epic and less cluttered aesthetic within the carousel, maintaining the rounded design and seamless loop.\n\n\n**Revisiting Marquee Mechanics**\n\nMy focus has sharpened on the underlying mechanics of the horizontal marquee. I\'m dissecting the CSS, specifically the `w-[200vw]` container. I\'m realizing that the current implementation\'s use of a single track containing both sets might lead to display issues with transitions. I\'m now exploring how adjusting the parent container can lead to a gapless, smoother marquee effect. I\'m keen on achieving a seamless `translateX(-50%)` animation.\n\n\n**Clarifying Marquee Dimensions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the interaction between container and track widths. I see that the `w-[200vw]` on the parent container is causing overflow and influencing layout. I\'m investigating how the marquee track\'s animation, and `-50%` translation, will interact with `w-screen`. I\'m testing `w-screen` for the parent, and I think that, with the help of a cloned set, it will ensure a true loop.\n\n\n**Optimizing Marquee Structure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the correct dimensions. I believe the outer container should be `w-full overflow-hidden`. The `w-[200vw]` on the inner wrapper seems unnecessary, potentially causing width issues and hindering the seamless loop. I\'m moving toward a standard Tailwind implementation: a `w-full overflow-hidden` container and a track that is `flex w-max`. I\'m confirming that `translateX(-50%)` will then work perfectly.\n\n\n**Calculating Marquee Coverage**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal card duplication strategy for full screen coverage and smooth repetition. Given the request for fewer columns (aiming for around 10 images), I\'m analyzing how to ensure a seamless loop across various screen sizes. I\'m exploring mapping the SHOWCASE_CARDS set multiple times. I\'m now testing mapping it 4 times for a more impactful presentation.\n\n\n**Simplifying Marquee Logic**\n\nI\'ve streamlined the marquee logic, opting for a clean approach: a parent container with `overflow-hidden` and a track animating `translateX(-50%)`. I\'ve realized two identical child sets of `SHOWCASE_CARDS` offer the cleanest seamless loop. Adding a third, and potentially a fourth, set ensures coverage on ultra-wide monitors.\n\n\n**Expanding Marquee Track**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the marquee\'s animation and track width for seamless loops on various screen sizes. I\'ve realized `-50%` translation requires a track wider than the screen. I\'m calculating the track width, confirming that two card sets are insufficient for wider displays, especially for a card set of only 6 cards. I\'m exploring increasing the track\'s content to ensure consistent screen coverage. I\'ll test expanding the card set content.\n\n\n**Curating Card Selection**\n\nI\'m now revisiting the source card data to curate a set of ten, elegant mockups. I\'m focusing on \"Less columns so its not so many images maybe only 10 images columns, smoothest marquee animation optimized and flowing smoothly\", the user\'s request. It seems the set of 6 cards in `SHOWCASE_CARDS` needs to be expanded or replaced. I\'m thinking of creating a 10-item set.\n\n\n**Perfecting Marquee Motion**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a completely jerk-free marquee animation. I\'ve realized the optimal solution involves a parent container with `w-full overflow-hidden select-none` and two identical child divs. Each child holds a complete set of cards, and they sit side-by-side in a flex row. The `animate-marquee` class, targeting a `-100%` `translateX` transformation, provides seamless repetition.\n\n\n**Verifying Marquee Mechanics**\n\nMy current focus is verifying the mathematical underpinnings of the marquee animation for jerk-free movement. I\'ve broken down the calculation to understand the perfect `-100%` transition for seamless repetition. The goal is to ensure both divs move perfectly with identical content. I\'m keen on establishing the relation between $W$ and the total visual behavior, including the zero reset!\n\n\n\n**Calculating Marquee Coverage**\n\nI\'m now diving into the impact of the `-50%` transition with an expanded marquee track. I\'ve realized, to achieve seamless repetition, the CSS keyframes need adjustment for a more precise transition to -100%. After some analysis, my current theory is that both divs, placed side-by-side inside a parent flex row, should animate from `translateX(0)` to `translateX(-100%)`.\n\n\n**Refining Marquee Animation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the marquee animation logic, specifically the `-100%` `translateX` transformation, and how it translates to the code. I\'m verifying the animation\'s current keyframes and considering if redefinition or a direct `@keyframes` modification in `/src/index.css` is best for the code base. I\'m reviewing the existing `@keyframes marquee-horizontal` and `@keyframes marquee-horizontal-reverse` to understand dependencies before proceeding with the `-50%` to `-100%` change. I\'m evaluating the implication of each approach.\n\n\n**Implementing Seamless Marquee**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final touches on the jerk-free marquee animation. My focus is on the `-100%` `translateX` modification within the CSS keyframes. I\'m verifying the current implementation, and I\'m updating the existing keyframe definitions (`marquee-horizontal` and `marquee-horizontal-reverse`) in `index.css` to enable `-100%` transitions on the dual tracks for a buttery-smooth effect. I\'m confident this will ensure flawless, seamless repetition. I\'m ensuring there are 10 total images being displayed. I\'m updating `SHOWCASE_CARDS` accordingly.\n\n\n\n**Curating Final Card Set**\n\nI\'ve finalized the curated set of ten showcase cards, ensuring each image embodies a high-end, modern marketing aesthetic with Unsplash images. I\'ve focused on minimal, abstract, geometric and graphic imagery for a cohesive and visually striking carousel. I\'ve swapped out the old `SHOWCASE_CARDS` with the new array, containing the updated card set. This set of 10 images will ensure a powerful presentation.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing Marquee Data**\n\nI\'ve just populated the `SHOWCASE_CARDS` array with the finalized set of ten unique, high-quality images. I\'ve focused on abstract, geometric, and graphically-driven visuals to create an immersive user experience. Each card has a title, metric, image URL, and associated technology for a compelling showcase. I\'m now testing the marquee with this newly populated set.\n\n\n**Verifying Image Integration**\n\nI\'m now verifying the seamless integration of the finalized ten showcase images within the marquee component. I\'m confirming that all images render correctly, maintain their aspect ratios, and contribute to a smooth visual loop. The focus is on a jerk-free animation, and all image cards are displaying. I\'m focusing on ensuring all the new cards are displayed within the final iteration of the marquee.\n\n\n**Confirming Marquee Setup**\n\nI\'ve just added the final showcase cards to the marquee component, completing the seamless carousel setup. I\'m excited by how easy it will be to implement. I\'m now reviewing the existing markup, and I\'m looking at how I will set up the code for the marquee HTML markup in the `App.tsx` file.\n\n\n**Refactoring Marquee Structure**\n\nI\'m now overhauling the marquee\'s HTML structure for optimal rendering. I\'m focusing on the `w-full` parent and the `flex w-full` child with `shrink-0`, which should prevent width overflow. I\'m also now replacing `w-[200vw]` with `w-full`, and keeping `overflow-hidden` for the wrapping container. I believe that this update, and the addition of `shrink-0` will ensure a cleaner implementation.\n\n\n**Optimizing Marquee HTML**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the marquee\'s HTML for the final update. I\'m focusing on simplifying and streamlining the markup for better readability and maintainability. I\'ve re-examined the code block and I\'m updating the spacing of elements to ensure visual clarity. I\'m reviewing the overall presentation of elements in `App.tsx` and focusing on the relationship between flexbox containers and image positioning. I\'m ensuring there are no unnecessary wrappers.\n\n\n**Revising Marquee Animation**\n\nI\'ve been re-evaluating the animation strategy and realized I need to revise the marquee animation\'s transformation values for ideal looping. I\'m focusing on the translation amount. I\'ve switched from a `-50%` to a `-100%` transformation value. I\'m confident that this minor adjustment will result in the perfect, seamless marquee effect with a complete card set width movement.\n\n\n**Confirming Marquee Transformation**\n\nI\'m now absolutely confirming the marquee animation logic. Based on recent analysis, I\'m confident that updating the CSS keyframes to a `-100%` transformation is the precise solution for a jerk-free marquee. I\'m updating both the `marquee-horizontal` and `marquee-horizontal-reverse` keyframes in `index.css`, and I\'m positive that both will result in a perfectly seamless effect. We have two tracks, with width $W$, and this ensures correct behavior.\n\n\n**Validating Marquee Movement**\n\nI\'m now validating the marquee\'s movement logic, confirming the smooth, jerk-free animation. I\'ve broken it down: two identical tracks, placed side-by-side inside a parent flex container. Each track has width W, so they have a total visual width of 2W (plus gaps). Animating each track from 0% to -100% translateX results in perfect looping. Track 1 ends off-screen where Track 2 begins, ensuring zero gap.\n\n\n"
