  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Animation Styles**\n\nI\'ve been working on defining the CSS animation for a marquee effect. Specifically, I\'ve created the `@keyframes marquee-horizontal` rule. It handles the left-to-right scrolling, with a 0% to 100% translateX transition. I\'ve been thinking about how this can be implemented in the App.tsx.\n\n\n**Verifying Seamless Transitions**\n\nI\'m now focused on achieving a flawless, shadow-free carousel. I\'ve been diving into the math to ensure seamless transitions between the marquee\'s dual-track rows. Ensuring the content and structure of both sets are precisely identical is proving key, as `translateX(-100%)` relies on equal widths. Specifically, I need to ensure there is no blank space when translating, so I am going to see how many images fit based on their specific widths.\n\n\n**Optimizing Infinite Loop Logic**\n\nI\'ve realized a critical issue: the `-100%` translation can break on ultra-wide screens if a single marquee row isn\'t wide enough. So, I\'m now implementing the 3-Set approach to guarantee seamless looping across all viewport sizes. Repeating the items twice within a track seems the simplest path, yielding a track width of 3312px, which is extremely safe.\n\n\n**Refining Translation Strategies**\n\nI\'m now revisiting the translation logic, considering `-50%` instead of `-100%`. I\'ve confirmed that if we double the array in both tracks, then animating by `-100%` works perfectly, with the second track seamlessly taking the first\'s place. I\'m focusing on ensuring the container is `flex w-full` in `App.tsx` and the marquee\'s shadow-free, dual-track rows are working as expected.\n\n\n**Analyzing Marquee Container Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating the container\'s impact on marquee animation, focusing on `w-full overflow-hidden whitespace-nowrap` styling. I\'m exploring the behavior of children sliding, specifically two sets which animate at `-100%`, and snap back. If the sets are identical, then the snap is invisible. I am also investigating the previously observed \"jerking\" behavior, and how that can be explained. I am reviewing the code, especially looking at how the duplicate container was previously implemented (or not).\n\n\n**Diagnosing the \"Jerk\"**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the \"jerk\": a single translating div vanished off-screen, creating a black void before looping. The solution? My updated code uses two divs. If the width of Set 1 is LESS than screen size, the 2nd will fill that space in the animation. This ensures a seamless transition. Now, I\'m verifying the animation\'s visual continuity.\n\n\n**Implementing Robust Sets**\n\nI\'m now focused on robustly addressing the blank-space jerk when a set is smaller than the screen. The key is ensuring each set is *at least* as wide as the widest expected screen. I\'m exploring an approach of repeating the item array, perhaps three times, within each set, which will be a very safe approach. The current math points to a minimum of 27 items to ensure good coverage.\n\n\n**Calculating Optimal Width**\n\nI\'ve determined that a width of 4698px guarantees flawless, gap-free marquee transitions, even on 4K screens. This approach provides incredibly smooth motion and is a robust, industry-standard solution. Now, I\'m verifying the image widths in the component. I\'m focusing on `w-[110px] h-[145px]` for mobile and `sm:w-[150px] sm:h-[195px]` for desktop. I\'ll evaluate the image density as a result of these sizes. Then, I\'ll update the image sizes for richness.\n\n\n**Evaluating Image Density**\n\nNow, I\'m working with the ShowcaseCard data to get more images for the marquee. I\'ve updated the `SHOWCASE_CARDS` array with the images. I\'m focusing on ensuring these images are correctly rendered within the marquees.\n\n\n**Refining Image Density**\n\nNow, I\'m working with the ShowcaseCard data to get more images for the marquee. I\'ve updated the `SHOWCASE_CARDS` array with the images. I\'m focusing on ensuring these images are correctly rendered within the marquees. I want to keep iterating on the image densities.\n\n\n**Validating Image URLs**\n\nI\'m now checking the image URLs to ensure they are all valid and correctly formatted. I\'ve verified each URL, confirming they all lead to the intended images.\n\n\n**Implementing Array Repeats**\n\nI\'m now implementing the array repeat, as a method of handling marquee transitions and ensuring it provides a clean, seamless loop. I\'ve built the `REPEATED_SHOWCASE` array. Currently, I am updating the `App.tsx` file, looking at the logic in lines 270-312 to make the changes I am thinking about. This involves modifying how the marquee tracks (Set 1 and Set 2) render, by iterating over `REPEATED_SHOWCASE`. I\'m verifying the 9 images are rendered three times.\n\n\n**Debugging Image Positioning**\n\nI\'m now focusing on debugging the positioning and rendering of the images within the marquee. I\'ve been reviewing the code snippet provided, paying close attention to the `className` attributes and their impact on image display. I\'m investigating the potential reasons why image positioning might not be aligned with expectations, and I\'m looking at potential problems that are occurring. I\'m especially focused on image `aspect` ratios and scaling.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now edit `src/App.tsx` to:\n1. Update `SHOWCASE_CARDS` to contain exactly 9 ultra-reliable and premium abstract design URLs.\n2. Dim the header\'s ambient red glowing backdrop elements slightly to make the layout more elegant.\n3. Make the marquee cards slightly larger (`w-[110px] h-[145px] sm:w-[150px] sm:h-[195px]` inside a taller container of `h-36 sm:h-48`).\n4. Triplicate the 9 showcase cards inside each sliding track to guarantee a complete seamless horizontal strip that covers any screen size without empty margins, eliminating the jerking/disappearance bug completely."
    thinking_duration {
